/usr/local/bin/boss park feat/a-refused-step-write-is-recorded \
  --backlog-item 7fddd448 \
  --summary "A refused step write is recorded. Step conformance measures 100% across 3,517 completed steps and cannot measure anything else — required-at-done validators run on the write path, so a step cannot reach completed without its required fields. The number certifies the validator, not the work; every real failure lives in the attempts that never became completions, and a refusal was an early return in http/steps.rs above the OUTBOX block, writing and emitting nothing. Refusals now land in step_write_refusals, recorded by ONE layer over the router rather than a call at each of steps.rs's ~15 refusal sites — that is a fact living fifteen times, and the next refusal added would silently go uncounted exactly where the protocol is newest." \
  --excludes "The count is NOT the metric and this deliberately ships no target for it: the cheapest way to drive refusals to zero is to loosen validation, which would destroy the one mechanism holding conformance at 100%. The two readings the indexes serve — refusals never followed by a success on that step, and distinct actors per (step, error_class) — are left as queries rather than baked into an endpoint until there is data to shape them. Also excluded: audit_log as the home for refusals (David chose a sibling table when the fork was put to him — the log records state changes and a refusal changes nothing), any recording of successful writes (that would make it a request log, not a measurement), 5xx (a server fault is not an actor failing to comply), and refused READS (a refused GET says nothing about whether an obligation was dischargeable)." \
  --test "6 unit tests over the pure classifier and route predicate, plus 5 integration tests through the real router with the in-memory adapter: a 409 on a completed step records class=state with the actor and both ids off the path; a write that lands records nothing; a refused GET records nothing; the recorded refusal is readable through its door; and — the one worth having — recording_does_not_damage_the_response_the_caller_reads, because the layer buffers the refusal body to read it and a mangled rebuild would cost every caller the message telling them what to fix. Full boss-jobs suite 293 + 25 pass. The error_class vocabulary lives in the Rust enum and the SQL CHECK, so per CLAUDE.md 9a it is pinned by the_class_vocabulary_matches_the_check_constraint and the migration names refusals.rs as the definition." \
  --verified "Gate green, verified on packet 30c19f71 rather than from the CLI line: receipt verdict=green, head=c8ccb133770f6507422a7d2438261213e9e897ce matching the commit, mode=full, fails=[]."
